1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:04,500
Right now I'm sitting in a neighborhood barber's chair, wrapped in a white cape.

2
00:00:04,567 --> 00:00:07,667
"Hold still. Turn your head now and your bangs are gone."

3
00:00:07,733 --> 00:00:10,400
Sure. Not that I have much in the way of bangs to begin with.

4
00:00:10,467 --> 00:00:14,467
One question from my son, and here we are. Let's rewind to last night.

5
00:00:15,033 --> 00:00:21,900
The question I dodged at the market came back last night in the shower, much more specific.

6
00:00:21,967 --> 00:00:24,533
"Dad, why do you have hair down there?"

7
00:00:24,600 --> 00:00:25,867
"I don't."

8
00:00:25,933 --> 00:00:28,033
All I could hear was the shower running.

9
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:31,167
"Uh... well... it grows when you're a grown-up."

10
00:00:31,233 --> 00:00:32,000
"Why?"

11
00:00:32,067 --> 00:00:35,233
"Uh... soap! Where'd the soap go?"

12
00:00:35,300 --> 00:00:39,833
I looked for the soap three times that night. It was in my hand the whole time.

13
00:00:39,900 --> 00:00:43,133
And today, it came up again. At the barbershop.

14
00:00:43,700 --> 00:00:47,267
"Grandpa! Why does my dad have hair down there?"

15
00:00:47,333 --> 00:00:50,400
"I asked him yesterday, but he just kept looking for the soap."

16
00:00:50,467 --> 00:00:52,900
"Ha. He looked for the soap?"

17
00:00:52,967 --> 00:00:58,233
"If it's about hair, you came to the right place. I've been cutting other people's hair for fifty years."

18
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:02,067
"Oh, sir, he's just a kid, he doesn't know..."

19
00:01:02,133 --> 00:01:05,467
Before I could finish, my kid went one step further.

20
00:01:06,033 --> 00:01:09,667
"Then why don't you have any hair on your head, Grandpa?"

21
00:01:09,733 --> 00:01:12,300
"Ha ha ha! Look at this one."

22
00:01:12,367 --> 00:01:15,933
"I'll tell you that one last. It's the best part."

23
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:19,567
The barbershop went quiet for the second time.

24
00:01:19,633 --> 00:01:25,200
While he went to warm up a towel, I pulled the cape over my head and turned on my phone.

25
00:01:25,267 --> 00:01:29,833
The creature known as Dad, when cornered, will search the internet even from under a barber's cape.

26
00:01:29,900 --> 00:01:35,133
It's understandable. We learned how many bones we have, but never why hair grows.

27
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:42,500
If anything we learned it as a feeling: something to shave, something embarrassing. So when a kid asks, 'shh' comes before any answer.

28
00:01:42,567 --> 00:01:51,133
Today, instead of shushing: three things. Why hair grows, why there of all places, and why it falls out in some places.

29
00:01:51,700 --> 00:01:55,567
"Come here, kiddo, give me your arm. Toward the window."

30
00:01:55,633 --> 00:02:00,400
In the sunlight you could see it. My kid's arm was covered in hair too.

31
00:02:00,467 --> 00:02:09,833
The human body has about five million hair follicles, almost everywhere except the palms, soles and lips. Not so different from a chimpanzee.

32
00:02:09,900 --> 00:02:13,900
"So it's not that you have no hair. It's just too fine to see."

33
00:02:14,467 --> 00:02:17,700
So why did humans trade thick hair for fine hair?

34
00:02:17,767 --> 00:02:26,667
The most common explanation is sweat: with fine hair, sweat dries fast, so we could walk and run for a long time even at midday. It's not settled.

35
00:02:26,733 --> 00:02:32,800
Either way, humanity traded fur for sweat. I mostly use that ability to catch buses.

36
00:02:33,367 --> 00:02:38,333
Some of the hair we kept has a clear job. Guard hair.

37
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:45,933
Head hair blocks the sun, eyebrows steer sweat to the sides, eyelashes are a dust alarm, and nose hair is a filter.

38
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:50,667
"That's why you don't pluck all your nose hair. You just trim the ones poking out."

39
00:02:51,233 --> 00:02:54,600
"And hair is an antenna. Close your eyes."

40
00:02:54,667 --> 00:02:56,600
"Hee hee! That tickles!"

41
00:02:56,667 --> 00:03:03,333
"I only touched the tips, and you felt it, right? Every hair root has a nerve wrapped around it. That's how you know when a mosquito lands."

42
00:03:03,900 --> 00:03:07,333
Every single hair is a tiny antenna.

43
00:03:07,400 --> 00:03:12,733
On the other hand, some hair seems to do almost nothing. Leftover hair.

44
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:21,567
Goosebumps happen because each follicle has a tiny muscle that raises the hair. A cat uses it to look twice its size. We just get bumpy skin.

45
00:03:21,633 --> 00:03:24,533
Only the machinery for raising fur is left.

46
00:03:25,100 --> 00:03:31,400
Then why does only head hair grow long? Not because it's thicker, but because it grows for longer.

47
00:03:31,467 --> 00:03:36,000
Head hair grows for two to seven years. Eyebrows stop after a few months.

48
00:03:36,067 --> 00:03:40,133
"If eyebrows grew like head hair, I'd be a rich man by now."

49
00:03:40,700 --> 00:03:46,267
Now, what my kid really asked. Why, when you grow up, does hair grow there of all places?

50
00:03:46,333 --> 00:03:49,367
"Let's do this one with a picture. On the mirror."

51
00:03:49,433 --> 00:03:55,000
He drew a person on the mirror in shaving foam, and then covered that part... with more foam.

52
00:03:55,067 --> 00:04:03,367
When the body is ready to grow up, hormones flow and the fine hair already there turns thick. It's not new hair. It's getting thicker.

53
00:04:03,433 --> 00:04:09,167
Usually somewhere between late elementary school and middle school. Early or late, both are normal.

54
00:04:09,733 --> 00:04:14,100
So why the armpits, and why down there? This is what surprised me most.

55
00:04:14,167 --> 00:04:19,733
There are two kinds of sweat glands. One is all over the body and pushes watery sweat straight onto the skin.

56
00:04:19,800 --> 00:04:24,167
The other kind is only in a few spots, like the armpits, the groin and the ear canal.

57
00:04:24,233 --> 00:04:28,067
And this kind doesn't open onto the skin. It opens into the hair follicle.

58
00:04:28,133 --> 00:04:39,133
The hair didn't come first. There's a special gland there and the hair is its partner, holding the sweat like a candle wick and slowly spreading it. Signal hair.

59
00:04:39,200 --> 00:04:49,800
This sweat barely smells on its own; the smell comes from skin bacteria that eat it. Exactly what it signals, scientists are still arguing about.

60
00:04:49,867 --> 00:04:55,067
And these glands switch on at puberty too. Hair and gland are a set.

61
00:04:55,633 --> 00:04:59,333
"Grandpa, you have loads of hair on your chest!"

62
00:04:59,400 --> 00:05:01,700
"You noticed? That's my pride and joy."

63
00:05:01,767 --> 00:05:04,267
"My dad doesn't have much chest hair."

64
00:05:04,333 --> 00:05:12,367
"That's about how sensitive the follicles are. The same hormone comes along, and some follicles jump at it while others couldn't care less."

65
00:05:12,933 --> 00:05:25,367
He's right. Chest hair depends less on hormone levels and more on how sensitive your follicles are and the genes you're born with. So it runs in families, and East Asians tend to have less body hair on average.

66
00:05:25,433 --> 00:05:29,500
Having more chest hair doesn't make you more manly, and having less doesn't make you less.

67
00:05:29,567 --> 00:05:30,800
"...Right, sir?"

68
00:05:30,867 --> 00:05:34,633
"Right. But you can still show it off."

69
00:05:35,200 --> 00:05:39,767
"Now, that question from before. Why don't I have hair on my head?"

70
00:05:39,833 --> 00:05:44,900
"Take a good look. Nothing on top, but the sides and back are fine, right?"

71
00:05:44,967 --> 00:05:50,333
The common kind of baldness is mostly the same hormone story.

72
00:05:50,900 --> 00:05:56,733
When male hormone turns into a stronger form in the body, it makes hair thicker on the chin and chest,

73
00:05:56,800 --> 00:06:02,633
but on the crown and front of the scalp it does the opposite, making hair finer and shorter until it's like fuzz.

74
00:06:02,700 --> 00:06:11,133
The sides and back barely respond, so a horseshoe is left. That's also why hair transplants use hair from the back.

75
00:06:11,200 --> 00:06:19,067
The person who showed this link was the American scientist James Hamilton in the 1940s, and the baldness stage chart still carries his name.

76
00:06:19,133 --> 00:06:27,233
The sensitivity is genetic. 'Look at your mother's father' is half right: one gene from the mother's side matters a lot, but many from the father's side mix in.

77
00:06:27,800 --> 00:06:32,733
"So when people say your head hair moved down to your chest, they're half right."

78
00:06:32,800 --> 00:06:37,000
Hearing that, I stared at the top of my own head in the mirror for a long time.

79
00:06:37,067 --> 00:06:45,933
"Shaving makes hair thicker? I've shaved for fifty years and it doesn't. The blunt tip just looks thicker."

80
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:49,533
A 1928 experiment found the same: no thicker.

81
00:06:50,100 --> 00:06:52,067
So, to sum up:

82
00:06:52,133 --> 00:06:54,500
Our hair didn't disappear. It got finer.

83
00:06:54,567 --> 00:07:01,333
And when the time comes, a different switch flips in each spot. Some spots get thicker, and some go the other way.

84
00:07:02,767 --> 00:07:08,600
After the haircut I stood outside with my kid, to give last night's answer.

85
00:07:08,667 --> 00:07:13,533
"Yesterday I just said it grows when you're a grown-up. That wasn't an answer."

86
00:07:13,600 --> 00:07:19,567
"Remember your arm in the sunlight? You have hair all over too. It's just very fine."

87
00:07:19,633 --> 00:07:26,800
"When your body is ready to grow up, that fine hair gets thicker in a few places. It's a sign your body is getting ready to become a grown-up body."

88
00:07:26,867 --> 00:07:35,633
"Why there? There's a special sweat gland there, and its sweat comes out through the hole the hair grows from. So the hair grows with it, as its partner."

89
00:07:35,700 --> 00:07:40,500
"It's not because it's dirty, and it's nothing to be embarrassed about. That's just how those places are made."

90
00:07:40,567 --> 00:07:42,867
"So when will mine get thick?"

91
00:07:42,933 --> 00:07:47,933
"I don't know. Your body knows. Early is fine, and late is fine."

92
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:49,000
"My body knows?"

93
00:07:49,067 --> 00:07:50,400
"Yep. Your body knows."

94
00:07:50,967 --> 00:07:57,467
"And let's make one promise. If you're curious about your body, ask me anytime. It's not an embarrassing question."

95
00:07:57,533 --> 00:08:07,067
"But we don't talk loudly about other people's bodies. Their bodies belong to them. Grandpa laughed today, but not everyone will."

96
00:08:07,133 --> 00:08:10,033
"So should I say sorry to Grandpa?"

97
00:08:10,100 --> 00:08:11,800
"Yeah. That'd be good."

98
00:08:11,867 --> 00:08:14,767
My kid opened the barbershop door and went back in.

99
00:08:15,333 --> 00:08:19,400
"Grandpa, I'm sorry I talked about your head so loudly."

100
00:08:19,467 --> 00:08:25,000
"Ha, that's alright. My head has been my pride for fifty years."

101
00:08:25,067 --> 00:08:29,967
"Next time, ask when it's just you and your dad. He'll answer well now."

102
00:08:30,533 --> 00:08:36,300
When your kid asks about their body, don't look for the soap. Try answering. It's more fun than you'd think.

103
00:08:36,367 --> 00:08:40,233
"Dad, so when will the hair on your head get thick again?"

104
00:08:40,300 --> 00:08:42,200
"...We'll be late for dinner. Let's hurry."

105
00:08:42,267 --> 00:08:43,933
"Dad, wait for me!"

106
00:08:44,500 --> 00:08:48,700
Next time: the question my kid asked when we went to the doctor with a cold.

107
00:08:48,767 --> 00:08:53,267
"What's the difference between a doctor, a pharmacist and a nurse?"

108
00:08:53,333 --> 00:08:55,833
Before then, I'll go do some more searching.
